What you’re seeing is normal: when you switch the PDF font to **Arial via XeLaTeX**, the **font metrics change** (Arial is wider than Computer Modern/Latin Modern in many sizes), so a table that *barely fit before* can now **overflow the text block**. And no—**LaTeX/kable does not auto-rescale tables to fit the page**. `kable()` just emits a LaTeX `tabular` inside a `table` float; LaTeX will happily let it run past the margins unless you explicitly tell it to resize, rotate, or wrap.

### Why it happens after Arial

* **Different glyph widths** → same number of characters takes more horizontal space.
* **Your table has many columns** (n, df_res, p, see, r2, adj_r2, rmse, mae, f_stat, f_p, aic, bic) → lots of fixed-width content.
* LaTeX does **not** automatically “shrink-to-fit” tabular content.

---

## Fixes (pick the one you like)

### 1) Auto-fit the table to page width (most SPSS-like “just make it fit”)

Use `kableExtra::kable_styling(latex_options="scale_down")`:

```r
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)

kable(tbl, format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE,
      caption = "LN_PRICE (base) model fit statistics") %>%
  kable_styling(latex_options = c("hold_position", "scale_down"))
```

This wraps the table in a resize box when needed.

> If you previously got “data is too long” errors: that’s usually when `kableExtra` thinks it’s parsing a markdown table. Make sure `format="latex"` and you’re knitting to PDF with a LaTeX engine.

---

### 2) Reduce the table font size only (often enough)

```r
kable(tbl, format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE) %>%
  kable_styling(latex_options = "hold_position", font_size = 7)
```

You can combine with `scale_down`:

```r
... %>% kable_styling(latex_options = c("hold_position", "scale_down"), font_size = 7)
```

---

### 3) Put it in landscape (best if you have a lot of columns and want readability)

```r
kable(tbl, format="latex", booktabs=TRUE) %>%
  kable_styling(latex_options = c("hold_position", "scale_down")) %>%
  landscape()
```

(Requires `kableExtra`; it will add the needed LaTeX bits.)

---

### 4) Make numeric columns narrower (formatting)

Long decimals waste width. Round aggressively and/or switch to scientific for AIC/BIC if needed:

```r
tbl2 <- tbl %>%
  mutate(across(where(is.numeric), ~ round(.x, 3)))

kable(tbl2, format="latex", booktabs=TRUE) %>%
  kable_styling(latex_options = c("hold_position", "scale_down"))
```

Also consider renaming headers shorter: `adj_r2` → `adjR2`, `f_stat` → `F`, etc.

---

### 5) Use `tabularx` to allow wrapping (rarely ideal for numeric tables)

This helps when you have long text columns; for numeric tables it’s usually worse. I’d only use it if you have text columns.

---

## Best “set it and forget it” pattern for wide tables

If you want one helper you can reuse:

```r
kable_fit <- function(x, caption = NULL, digits = 3, font_size = 8) {
  x2 <- x
  num_cols <- vapply(x2, is.numeric, logical(1))
  x2[num_cols] <- lapply(x2[num_cols], round, digits = digits)

  knitr::kable(x2, format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE, caption = caption) %>%
    kableExtra::kable_styling(
      latex_options = c("hold_position", "scale_down"),
      font_size = font_size
    )
}
```

Then:

```r
kable_fit(tbl, "LN_PRICE (base) model fit statistics")
```

