Yes — in PDF/LaTeX you can put two `kable` tables **side-by-side** by wrapping them in **minipage** blocks (or `kableExtra::column_spec` etc.). The cleanest/least fragile approach is:

1. render each table as LaTeX (`format="latex"`)
2. capture the LaTeX as a string
3. print two `minipage`s on one line

Below is a drop-in helper that takes **two already-built VIF tibbles** (or data frames) and prints them side-by-side with independent captions.

> This works best when each table is narrow (like your 3-column VIF/tolerance tables).

---

## Side-by-side kable for two VIF tables (PDF / LaTeX)

````r
```{r vif-side-by-side, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='asis'}
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(dplyr)

kable_vif_latex <- function(vif_df, caption = NULL, digits = 3, font_size = 7) {

  vif_df <- vif_df %>%
    mutate(across(where(is.numeric), ~ round(.x, digits)))

  knitr::kable(
    vif_df,
    format   = "latex",
    booktabs = TRUE,
    caption  = caption,
    align    = c("l", "r", "r")
  ) %>%
    kableExtra::kable_styling(
      latex_options = c("hold_position"),
      font_size = font_size,
      full_width = FALSE
    )
}

# ---- Example: replace these with YOUR two tables produced by your VIF function ----
# vif_tbl_1 <- vif_out1  # term, vif, tolerance
# vif_tbl_2 <- vif_out2

tab1 <- kable_vif_latex(vif_tbl_1, caption = "VIF / GVIF (Model 1)")
tab2 <- kable_vif_latex(vif_tbl_2, caption = "VIF / GVIF (Model 2)")

# Convert to raw latex strings for minipage layout
tab1_tex <- as.character(tab1)
tab2_tex <- as.character(tab2)

cat("
\\noindent
\\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\\linewidth}
\\centering
", tab1_tex, "
\\end{minipage}
\\hfill
\\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\\linewidth}
\\centering
", tab2_tex, "
\\end{minipage}
")
````

````

### Notes
