`predict()` returning **`NA`** (instead of throwing an error) almost always means:

1. **At least one predictor in `ndt` is `NA`/`NaN`** (or sometimes `Inf`), **or**
2. `ndt` has a **factor level mismatch** vs what `mod_tasp` was trained on (often prints a warning like “factor has new levels …”, and the model matrix ends up with `NA`s).

`predict.lm()` will happily return `NA` for that row when the constructed model matrix contains `NA`s.

---

## 1) First: verify you actually got one row back

```r
nrow(ndt)
ndt %>% dplyr::select(saleid, dplyr::everything()) %>% dplyr::glimpse()
```

If `nrow(ndt) == 0`, you’d normally get `numeric(0)`, not `NA`, so you likely have 1 row — but confirm.

---

## 2) Identify *exactly which* variables are causing the `NA`

The cleanest way is to ask the model what variables it expects, then check `ndt` for missing/non-finite values.

```r
resp <- as.character(formula(mod_tasp))[2]
needed <- setdiff(names(model.frame(mod_tasp)), resp)

# Which needed vars are NA?
na_vars <- needed[vapply(ndt[needed], function(x) any(is.na(x) | is.nan(x)), logical(1))]

# Which needed vars are Inf/-Inf?
inf_vars <- needed[vapply(ndt[needed], function(x) any(is.infinite(x)), logical(1))]

na_vars
inf_vars

# Show the actual problematic values
ndt %>% dplyr::select(dplyr::any_of(unique(c(na_vars, inf_vars))))
```

If `na_vars` is non-empty, that’s your answer: your sale row has missing predictor(s) used by the model.

### Common culprits in your workflow

* Any `ln_*` variable where the raw value was 0 or negative and was logged (creates `-Inf`, then often becomes `NA` downstream).
* A dummy block where none (or multiple) flags are set for that row.
* A field that exists in the dataset but is blank for that specific sale.

---

## 3) Check factor level mismatches (very common)

Even if there are no `NA`s, a factor mismatch can still introduce `NA`s when the model matrix is built.

```r
xlev <- .getXlevels(terms(mod_tasp), model.frame(mod_tasp))
fac_vars <- intersect(names(xlev), names(ndt))

bad_levels <- lapply(fac_vars, function(v) {
  val <- as.character(ndt[[v]])
  if (length(val) == 0) return(NULL)
  if (is.na(val) || !(val %in% xlev[[v]])) val else NULL
})
bad_levels <- bad_levels[!vapply(bad_levels, is.null, logical(1))]
bad_levels
```

Also check if `predict()` is warning you but you didn’t notice:

```r
predt <- predict(mod_tasp, newdata = ndt)
warnings()
predt
```

If you see “new levels”, you need to align factor levels.

---

## 4) Fixes (pick the one that matches what you find)

### Fix A: Align factor levels to training (safe, recommended)

```r
mf_train <- model.frame(mod_tasp)
xlev <- .getXlevels(terms(mod_tasp), mf_train)

for (v in names(xlev)) {
  if (v %in% names(ndt)) {
    ndt[[v]] <- factor(as.character(ndt[[v]]), levels = xlev[[v]])
  }
}

predt <- predict(mod_tasp, newdata = ndt)
predt
```

If a value is truly unseen, it will become `NA` → prediction `NA`, which is correct unless you want an explicit fallback.

---

### Fix B: Your `ln_garagespaces` / log transforms created `-Inf`

If `inf_vars` includes any `ln_*` column (especially garage-related), rebuild those as `log1p()`:

```r
df <- df %>%
  dplyr::mutate(
    ln_garagespaces = log1p(garagespaces)  # safe for 0
  )
```

Then re-fit the model (or at least regenerate `ndt` from the updated `df`).

---

### Fix C: Impute missing predictors for single-row prediction (practical)

If you want a prediction even when a row has missing values, impute from the training data:

```r
impute_newdata_like_training <- function(mod, newdata) {
  mf_train <- model.frame(mod)
  resp <- as.character(formula(mod))[2]
  needed <- setdiff(names(mf_train), resp)

  # Keep only what the model needs (optional but helps)
  newdata <- newdata[, needed, drop = FALSE]

  # Align factor levels
  xlev <- .getXlevels(terms(mod), mf_train)
  for (v in names(xlev)) {
    if (v %in% names(newdata)) {
      newdata[[v]] <- factor(as.character(newdata[[v]]), levels = xlev[[v]])
    }
  }

  # Impute numerics w/ median, factors w/ mode
  for (v in needed) {
    if (!v %in% names(newdata)) next
    if (is.numeric(mf_train[[v]])) {
      bad <- is.na(newdata[[v]]) | is.nan(newdata[[v]]) | is.infinite(newdata[[v]])
      if (any(bad)) newdata[[v]][bad] <- stats::median(mf_train[[v]], na.rm = TRUE)
    } else if (is.factor(mf_train[[v]])) {
      bad <- is.na(newdata[[v]])
      if (any(bad)) {
        mode_lvl <- names(sort(table(mf_train[[v]]), decreasing = TRUE))[1]
        newdata[[v]][bad] <- mode_lvl
        newdata[[v]] <- factor(newdata[[v]], levels = levels(mf_train[[v]]))
      }
    }
  }

  newdata
}

ndt2 <- impute_newdata_like_training(mod_tasp, ndt)
predict(mod_tasp, newdata = ndt2)
```

That will produce a numeric prediction unless something is structurally broken.

